<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     TIME DIFFERENCE SECTION & SIDE-BY-SIDE GRAPHIC
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section" id="time-difference" aria-labelledby="time-diff-heading">
  <div class="method-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Time Difference Mechanics</p>
    <h2 id="time-diff-heading">Analyzing the Time Gap: EEST vs EST</h2>
    <p>
      The time difference between Eastern European Summer Time (EEST) and Eastern Standard Time (EST) is exactly <strong>8 hours</strong>. Since EEST operates at UTC+3 and EST is fixed at UTC-5, EEST runs eight hours ahead of Eastern Standard Time. When it is 8:00 AM EST in New York during winter, local clocks in Athens and Helsinki show 4:00 PM EEST.
    </p>
    <p>
      This time difference shifts when standard winter time is active. In winter, Eastern Europe switches to Eastern European Time (EET, UTC+2) and Eastern North America is on EST (UTC-5), resulting in a 7-hour difference. During summer, when both regions are on daylight saving, Europe is on EEST (UTC+3) and the US is on Eastern Daylight Time (EDT, UTC-4), maintaining a 7-hour difference. The 8-hour difference only occurs during the brief transition weeks in March and October when one region has switched but the other has not.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Custom EEST vs EST Comparison Infographic (SVG) -->
  <div style="margin: 2rem auto; max-width: 700px; padding: 1.5rem; background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 12px;">
