<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     COMMON MISTAKES & CONFUSIONS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="content" id="common-mistakes" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Confusions and Pitfalls in EEST to EST Calculations</h2>
  <article>
    <h3>Confusing EEST with EET</h3>
    <p>
      EEST is the summer time offset (UTC+3), while EET is the winter standard time (UTC+2). Using the wrong offset when calculating time differences can result in a 1-hour scheduling error. Always verify the calendar date before applying the offset.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>Eastern Time vs Eastern Standard Time</h3>
    <p>
      "Eastern Time" is a general term that refers to both EST (winter, UTC-5) and EDT (summer, UTC-4). If you assume Eastern Time is always EST, your meetings during summer months will be misaligned. Use our DST-aware calculator to determine the correct offset.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>Assuming Europe and the US transition DST on the same day</h3>
    <p>
      The US transitions to daylight saving time in mid-March, whereas Europe transitions on the last Sunday of March. In autumn, the US transitions in early November, while Europe transitions in late October. During these transition weeks, the relative time gap shifts by 1 hour.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

